# Line-ending policy.
#
# 2026-07-30. This file exists because its absence had silently disabled
# the nightly Postgres backup.
#
# The dev box runs Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, so git checks every
# text file out as CRLF. Normally that is invisible: git converts back on
# commit, and CI/Linux only ever see the LF version in the object store.
#
# It is NOT invisible here, because `scripts/deploy.ts` ships the WORKING
# TREE — it tars the checked-out directory rather than using `git
# archive`. So whatever line endings are on the Windows disk are the line
# endings that land on the production box. `infra/backup/compass-backup.sh`
# arrived with a `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` shebang, and systemd reported:
#
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# `compass-backup.service` had been failing on every timer fire, which
# means there were no nightly database backups at all.
#
# Everything the Linux box executes or parses is pinned to LF below.
# `text=auto` keeps normal source files behaving as before.
* text=auto

# Executed by a shell on the server — a CR in the shebang is fatal.
*.sh text eol=lf

# systemd refuses to parse units with stray CRs in some directives, and
# ExecStart values silently inherit the CR.
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer text eol=lf

# Read by Linux daemons / builders.
Caddyfile text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Binary-ish assets git should never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
